I mean what meanwhile have expressed other people, e.g. quoting Kent, in
ways like:
   "Instead, as every reasonably sane participant in
   NetHack realizes, a game which throws stuff and
   situations at the player randomly requires great
   flexibility for success."

HPs and AC are no exceptions.

An experienced player can choose his strategies to optimize the overall
gain, e.g. he can try the Mines early if his specific character is ready
for that, or try fountain wishes if he can handle the outcome, or one of
many other options. But what ever factor(s) a player decides to solve in
any instance of game-time is very much depending on how the random game
evolved and on the type of character played.

> If I glom up all the potions of
> *healing available in a typical game, without searching Gehennom, I can
> easily generate enough PoFH to get 300HP.

I requote my earlier statement...
   "Strangely enough my games differ so much that I don't always have the
   desired HP:300 AC:-30; and here I am *not* talking about the late game
   where I often abstain from drinking all !PoFH."

I was *not* talking about the late game.

> Since even pre-alchemy
> characters could score 150HP, that only demands circa 20 potions of
> *healing; around 11% of potions are *healing, and they are a common object
> type [...]

That's why I've written "(Sometimes I have the impression we're playing
different games.)".

In *my* games it's quite common that until the Castle (and sometimes even
until the VS level) even "common" objects, i.e. objects of high generation
probability, are not even generated in a single instance. The only reliable
items are the guaranteed ones; the Castle wand gives the opportunity to
balance - to a certain degree, and depending on what the RNG created in any
specific game - that.

One additional factor adding to what a character has are shops (and bones).
In some games I get an abundance of shops and I stumble across half a dozen
bones heaps, while in other games even the guaranteed minetown shops are
variants where only the minimum amount of random shops are generated, not
a single bones file loaded, and finally the castle wand turns out to have
one charge, rarely a compensation for all the missing items.

Are these exceptional or rare circumstances? No, not in my games. That's
why I am differing from some other posters and still consider Nethack to
be a very challenging game with enough variations to be interesting even
in cases where one is ascending every other game.

